          "file_path": "plugins/pretty-link/app/controllers/PrliAppController.php",
          "line_number": 132,
          "note": "The callback exits unless `PrliUtils::is_authorized()` is true."
        },
        {
          "file_path": "plugins/pretty-link/app/controllers/PrliAppController.php",
          "line_number": 137,
          "note": "User input is reduced with `sanitize_key()` before use."
        },
        {
          "file_path": "plugins/pretty-link/app/controllers/PrliAppController.php",
          "line_number": 138,
          "note": "The persistent key is always prefixed as `prli_dismiss_notice_$notice`."
        },
        {
          "file_path": "plugins/pretty-link/app/controllers/PrliAppController.php",
          "line_number": 152,
          "note": "The daily-dismiss path writes a transient using the same fixed prefix."
        },
        {
          "file_path": "plugins/pretty-link/app/controllers/PrliAppController.php",
          "line_number": 166,
          "note": "The monthly-dismiss path also writes only a plugin-scoped transient."
        }
      ],
      "reasoning": {
        "hypothesis": "Semgrep's `update_option(\"prli_dismiss_notice_$notice\", true)` hit could let an attacker write arbitrary WordPress options and escalate privileges.",
        "trace": "The three dismiss callbacks take `$_POST['notice']`, verify a nonce, require an authorized plugin user, reduce the value with `sanitize_key()`, and then write only notice-related options or transients whose names start with `prli_dismiss_notice_`.",
        "defenses": "The nonce and `PrliUtils::is_authorized()` checks gate entry. The sink names are constrained by a fixed prefix, and the resulting state change only affects Pretty Links notice visibility.",
        "refutation": "I could not make user input escape the hard-coded `prli_dismiss_notice_` prefix or influence a sensitive core option such as `default_role` or `users_can_register`. Even a successful call only suppresses plugin notices, which is not a meaningful security boundary in this context.",
        "conclusion": "Rejected as a false positive. The pattern looks like an option update, but the reachable impact is limited to plugin-local notice state."
      },
      "reopen_if": "Show a way to break out of the hard-coded prefix or a notice key whose suppression changes access control or discloses data."
    },
    {
      "finding_id": "NEG-004",
      "state": "REJECTED",
      "verdict": "FP",
      "title": "Bookmarklet route is gated by a secret token and does not yield a practical unauthenticated create-link or SSRF primitive",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "file_path": "plugins/pretty-link/app/models/PrliOptions.php",
          "line_number": 342,
          "note": "The bookmarklet secret is generated once as `md5(get_option('auth_salt') . time())` and stored in plugin options."
        },
        {
          "file_path": "plugins/pretty-link/app/models/PrliLink.php",
          "line_number": 585,
          "note": "The bookmarklet URL embeds `k={$prli_options->bookmarklet_auth}` as a bearer token."
        },
        {
          "file_path": "plugins/pretty-link/app/views/tools/form.php",
          "line_number": 26,
          "note": "That bookmarklet URL is presented on the Tools page, which is part of the authorized admin UI."
        },
        {
          "file_path": "plugins/pretty-link/app/controllers/PrliToolsController.php",
          "line_number": 34,
          "note": "`standalone_route()` only continues when `$_GET['k'] == $prli_options->bookmarklet_auth`; otherwise it redirects home."
        },
        {
          "file_path": "plugins/pretty-link/app/controllers/PrliToolsController.php",
          "line_number": 53,
          "note": "Only after the token check does the route call `prli_create_pretty_link()` with the requested target URL."
        },
        {
          "file_path": "plugins/pretty-link/app/models/PrliLink.php",
          "line_number": 59,
          "note": "Link creation auto-fetches a page title via `PrliUtils::get_page_title()` when no explicit name is supplied."
        },
        {
          "file_path": "plugins/pretty-link/app/models/PrliUtils.php",
          "line_number": 856,
          "note": "`get_page_title()` performs a server-side `wp_remote_get($url, ...)` only after the bookmarklet token gate or another authorized link-creation flow."
        }
      ],
      "reasoning": {
        "hypothesis": "Because `standalone_route()` is reachable on the public frontend, an unauthenticated attacker could create Pretty Links or trigger server-side fetches to arbitrary URLs.",
        "trace": "The public `action=prli_bookmarklet` route checks `$_GET['k']` against the stored `bookmarklet_auth` secret before it calls `bookmark()`, and `bookmark()` then invokes `prli_create_pretty_link()`. If the new link has no title, the create flow fetches the target URL to extract a page title.",
        "defenses": "The route is not open by default; it requires possession of the secret bookmarklet token. That token is generated from the site's auth salt and time and is exposed by the shipped plugin only on the authorized Tools page.",
        "refutation": "I did not find a bundled path that leaks `bookmarklet_auth` to unauthenticated users or to roles below the plugin's authorized UI boundary. Without that secret, the route just redirects home. That makes the create-link and page-title-fetch behavior theoretical for an unauthenticated attacker, not a demonstrated exploit path.",
        "conclusion": "Rejected as a false positive. The bookmarklet is a token-gated feature, not a default unauthenticated create-link or SSRF endpoint."
      },
      "reopen_if": "Show a bundled low-privileged or public surface that exposes `bookmarklet_auth`, or demonstrate a feasible token prediction/collision attack against the generated value."
    },
    {
      "finding_id": "NEG-005",
      "state": "REJECTED",
      "verdict": "FP",
      "title": "Public invoice rendering requires possession of a Stripe checkout session ID and does not expose a practical low-privilege data leak",
      "entry_point_ids": [
        "ep_init_init"
      ],
      "evidence": [
        {
          "file_path": "plugins/pretty-link/app/controllers/PrliStripeController.php",
          "line_number": 884,
          "note": "`display_invoice()` only activates when `$_GET['prli_session_id']` exists and begins with `cs_`."
        },
        {
          "file_path": "plugins/pretty-link/app/controllers/PrliStripeController.php",
          "line_number": 899,
          "note": "The session ID is then used to retrieve the Stripe checkout session from the site's own Stripe account."
        },
        {
          "file_path": "plugins/pretty-link/app/views/prettypay/invoice.php",
          "line_number": 9,
          "note": "The rendered output contains payment status and the payment intent ID, not customer credentials."
        },
        {
          "file_path": "plugins/pretty-link/app/views/prettypay/invoice.php",
          "line_number": 29,
          "note": "The line-item table exposes descriptions, prices, subtotal, tax, and total."
        },
        {
          "file_path": "plugins/pretty-link/app/views/prettypay/invoice.php",
          "line_number": 59,
          "note": "For subscriptions it links only to the site's customer-portal page, not directly to an authenticated Stripe portal session."
        }
      ],
      "reasoning": {
        "hypothesis": "Because the invoice view is public and lacks a nonce or capability check, any attacker can read arbitrary Stripe checkout data by supplying `prli_session_id`.",
        "trace": "A public page load with `prli_session_id=cs_...` reaches `PrliStripeController::display_invoice()`, which asks Stripe for that session and appends the rendered invoice template to the page content.",
        "defenses": "The only selector is the Stripe checkout session ID itself. The plugin does not expose a route to enumerate session IDs, and the rendered template omits customer email, payment method details, and other high-sensitivity account data.",
        "refutation": "I found no bundled path that leaks arbitrary checkout session IDs to low-privileged users, and I found no evidence that the plugin makes those IDs predictable or enumerable. The success URL intentionally carries the session ID back to the paying user, so possession of that value is already the gating secret for the lightweight invoice summary the plugin renders.",
        "conclusion": "Rejected as a false positive for Patchstack-style reporting. The code could be hardened further, but I do not have an evidence-backed low-privilege or unauthenticated disclosure primitive."
      },

