        "hypothesis": "The Stripe connect AJAX endpoints might let any logged-in user update arbitrary WordPress options or force the plugin to adopt attacker-controlled Stripe credentials.",
        "trace": "`process_update_creds()` and `process_refresh_tokens()` both start with nonce checks, then require `PrliUtils::is_authorized()`, then fetch remote data using the fixed service method ID, and finally store the returned values in fixed Pretty Links Stripe options.",
        "defenses": "Each AJAX action is `wp_ajax_` only, has a dedicated nonce, and enforces the plugin's authorization boundary. The stored option names are constant strings; only the values returned by the remote service vary.",
        "refutation": "I did not find any path where a subscriber or unauthenticated actor can reach these sinks. I also did not find any path where request input controls the option key or remote host. The `pmt` parameter only gates early execution and does not become the method ID used for the remote call.",
        "conclusion": "Rejected as a false positive. The code is updating plugin-owned Stripe configuration after authenticated, nonce-protected service interactions, not exposing a generic option-update primitive."
      },
