docs/bug-bounty-rules-summary.md:340:## Patchstack - phù hợp khi
docs/bug-bounty-rules-summary.md:351:- **Patchstack**: WordPress core là in scope.
docs/bug-bounty-rules-summary.md:354:- **Patchstack** nhấn mạnh mạnh vào tiêu chí public distribution, active installs, quyền khai thác, và uniqueness.
docs/wp-context.md:3:**This is a comprehensive, technically precise reference for auditing WordPress 6.x plugins**, synthesizing real CVE data from 2023–2025, official WordPress developer documentation, and research from Wordfence, Patchstack, and WPScan. The WordPress plugin ecosystem saw **7,966 new vulnerabilities in 2024 alone** (a 34% increase over 2023), with XSS accounting for 47.7% of all findings, broken access control at 14.2%, and CSRF at 11.4%. REST API and AJAX endpoints are the most critically exploited attack surfaces. What follows is every detail a professional security auditor needs.
docs/wp-context.md:197:**CVE-2024-28000 — LiteSpeed Cache privilege escalation (CVSS 9.8)**. The crawler simulation feature used `mt_rand()` seeded with microsecond time to generate security hashes — producing only ~1 million possible values. An unauthenticated AJAX endpoint (`async_litespeed`) had no capability or nonce checks, allowing anyone to trigger hash generation. The hash could then be brute-forced within hours to create admin accounts via the REST API.
docs/wp-context.md:246:**Subscriber, contributor, and author all map to PR:Low** — a significant loss of nuance. Patchstack explicitly notes that "CVSS score is a way to evaluate and rank reported vulnerabilities in a standardized and repeatable way but which is not ideal for WordPress."
docs/wp-context.md:259:- **Patchstack** assigns CVEs and CVSS scores but marks them "Low" priority in their proprietary system
docs/wp-context.md:262:### Patchstack Priority system vs raw CVSS
docs/wp-context.md:264:Patchstack created a WordPress-specific priority system in November 2023, acknowledging that **64.7% of WordPress vulnerabilities receive "Medium" CVSS scores** (even when requiring high privileges and unlikely to be exploited in practice). Their system categorizes into three levels:
docs/wp-context.md:274:Patchstack and Wordfence are both CVE Numbering Authorities (CNAs). In 2025, **Patchstack became the #1 CNA globally** with 7,007 CVEs assigned — more than Microsoft or Google. Together, Patchstack and Wordfence account for 10,000+ combined CVEs, making WordPress plugin security the dominant category of CVE issuance worldwide. All three major WordPress security databases (Wordfence, Patchstack, WPScan) use **CVSS v3.1** — none have adopted CVSS v4.0 as of early 2026.
docs/wp-context.md:423:- https://patchstack.com/database/ — Patchstack vulnerability database
docs/wp-context.md:453:- https://patchstack.com/articles/ — Patchstack research articles
docs/wp-context.md:475:The WordPress plugin security landscape in 2023–2025 reveals that **the same vulnerability patterns recur with striking consistency** across plugins of all sizes. Missing authorization checks on AJAX/REST endpoints, insufficient output escaping, and broken authentication logic in REST API permission callbacks account for the vast majority of critical vulnerabilities. The REST API has displaced traditional AJAX as the primary attack surface for critical-severity issues, while contributor-level Stored XSS via block attributes and shortcodes dominates the volume statistics.
docs/wp-context.md:479:For security auditors, the priority matrix is clear: **audit all AJAX and REST API endpoints first** (check every handler for both nonce verification AND capability checking), then review all output contexts for proper escaping (especially `render_callback` in blocks and shortcode output), then examine authentication/authorization logic in custom login/registration flows, and finally review file operations and database queries for injection vulnerabilities. The Patchstack Priority system's finding that only 11.6% of vulnerabilities are truly high-risk (versus CVSS's 34.8% high/critical classification) should guide triage decisions in large audit engagements.
docs/wp-context.md:483:# Patchstack bug bounty program: complete rules and guidelines
docs/wp-context.md:485:**Patchstack operates the WordPress ecosystem's largest bug bounty program, with rules that diverge sharply from generic vulnerability programs by enforcing WordPress-specific thresholds for CVSS scores, user roles, install counts, and vulnerability impact.** The current ruleset — the "2026 edition" updated February 1, 2026 — spans 26 sections governing everything from scope and scoring to payouts up to **$33,000** per vulnerability. The program uses CVSS v3.1 base scores as its primary severity metric but layers on a proprietary "Patchstack Priority" system that classifies real-world exploitability into High, Medium, and Low tiers. Understanding these rules is essential for researchers: roughly **69.6%** of all reported vulnerabilities receive Low priority (considered theoretical), and the program explicitly rejects admin-only bugs, low-CVSS issues, and several common vulnerability classes outright.
docs/wp-context.md:489:## Scope: what Patchstack accepts and what it rejects
docs/wp-context.md:497:A component must have **100 or more active installs** to qualify, with two narrow exceptions. First, components enrolled in an active Patchstack mVDP (managed Vulnerability Disclosure Program) are accepted regardless of install count. Second, vulnerabilities scoring **CVSS 8.0+** in components with at least **50 active installs** qualify if the prerequisite is not admin or higher. Components whose last version was released more than **three years ago** are out of scope, as are vulnerabilities that no longer exist in the latest version (silently patched but never disclosed).
docs/wp-context.md:504:- **CVSS below 5.3**: Not accepted for direct submission, with one exception pathway (vendor confirms the issue and requests Patchstack CVE assignment) and one threshold reduction (CVSS **4.3+** accepted if the component has **100,000+ active installs** or an active mVDP program)
docs/wp-context.md:506:- **Arbitrary user registration**: Rejected unless it results in creation of an arbitrary administrator account
docs/wp-context.md:521:Patchstack accepts XSS at all non-admin privilege levels. **Contributor+ Stored XSS** is the most commonly reported type in the WordPress ecosystem, typically exploiting shortcode, Gutenberg block, or Elementor widget implementations where a Contributor creates a drafted post with an XSS payload and sends a preview link to privileged users. Subscriber+ and Unauthenticated Stored XSS are accepted and valued progressively higher. Reflected XSS is accepted at all non-admin levels but scores lower on CVSS due to the user interaction requirement. Notably, the shortcode preview exclusion (Rule 3.4) specifically exempts XSS — XSS via shortcode previews **is** accepted even when other shortcode preview issues would be rejected. For testing Editor+ or Admin+ XSS, researchers must disable `unfiltered_html` via `DISALLOW_UNFILTERED_HTML` in wp-config.php, since WordPress grants this capability to Administrators and Editors by default on single-site installations.
docs/wp-context.md:529:SQLi receives a **x2 XP multiplier** and is accepted at all non-admin privilege levels. Patchstack notes that WordPress's `wp_magic_quotes()` auto-escapes inputs, making many seemingly vulnerable code paths unexploitable in practice — researchers must demonstrate actual exploitability. Common vectors include improper `$wpdb->prepare()` usage, direct string concatenation in SQL queries, and ORDER BY clause injection. Unauthenticated SQLi leading to full site compromise qualifies for Zeroday bounties.
docs/wp-context.md:549:## CVSS scoring and Patchstack Priority: a dual system
docs/wp-context.md:553:Patchstack uses **only the CVSS v3.1 base score** (not Temporal or Environmental scores) for all calculations, computed via the official FIRST calculator. They explicitly acknowledge that "CVSS score is a way to evaluate and rank reported vulnerabilities in a standardized and repeatable way, but it is not ideal for WordPress/CMSs." To compensate, they add WordPress-specific context — most importantly mapping the "Privileges Required" vector to WordPress roles (Unauthenticated = "None"; any logged-in user including Subscriber = "Low"). The severity bands used in the Patchstack dashboard are: **Low** (0.0–3.9), **Medium** (4.0–6.9), **High** (7.0–8.9), and **Critical** (9.0+).
docs/wp-context.md:555:### Patchstack Priority: the real-world exploitability layer
docs/wp-context.md:557:Introduced in November 2023, Patchstack Priority supplements CVSS with dynamic, WordPress-specific threat assessment. **High Priority** vulnerabilities are expected to become actively mass-exploited or are already known to be exploited — they receive a virtual patching (vPatch) rule and carry a recommended time-to-patch of **0 days**. **Medium Priority** vulnerabilities could be exploited in targeted attacks, receive a vPatch rule, and carry a **7-day** patching window. **Low Priority** vulnerabilities are "not expected to become exploited," are considered "often theoretical," do **not** receive a vPatch, and carry a **30-day** patching window. In 2024 data, **11.6%** received High priority, **18.8%** Medium, and **69.6%** Low — meaning Patchstack Priority helps users cut noise by roughly 70% compared to CVSS alone.
docs/wp-context.md:571:All vulnerabilities must be new and unique — not previously reported or publicly disclosed. If multiple researchers report the same vulnerability (even across different parameters or endpoints), **credit goes to the first valid submission only**. Attempting to manipulate this through multiple accounts or cross-program duplicate submissions can result in account suspension. One exception exists: vulnerabilities first reported directly to the vendor before Patchstack submission are accepted if the vendor confirms and indicates willingness to patch. This is **mandatory** for Automattic components, which must first go through Automattic's HackerOne program.
docs/wp-context.md:573:Incomplete patches that are publicly disclosed are **not** considered new vulnerabilities. However, if an incomplete patch introduces a **genuinely new attack vector** not previously possible, it may qualify as a new submission. Researchers get **up to two chances** to fix and resubmit rejected reports. Three rejected submissions within one month triggers suspension for the remainder of that month plus the following month. Repeated AI-generated false-positive submissions trigger a one-month cooldown; continued abuse results in permanent account lock.
docs/wp-context.md:595:Patchstack validates submitted vulnerabilities within **72 hours** of receipt. After validation, the vendor is contacted through the most efficient publicly listed contact channels — Patchstack will not create accounts for vendor support forums or ticketing systems. The standard disclosure window is **30 days** from the first vendor contact attempt. If a vendor fails to respond within **7 days**, Patchstack reserves the right to disclose immediately. The bug bounty rules add that if a vendor does not respond within **14 days**, Patchstack may proceed with public disclosure and notify relevant security teams. For mVDP-enrolled components, timelines may be extended, while disclosure may be **accelerated** in cases of active exploitation or third-party disclosure.
docs/wp-context.md:597:Starting with the 2026 edition, all publications comply with **EU Cyber Resilience Act** requirements: disclosure is instant once the patch is released and validated. Patches must be released as dedicated security-only updates with no bundled code modifications, and the version number or changelog must clearly indicate a security fix. Researchers are prohibited from disclosing vulnerability details to any third party before official Patchstack public disclosure (when the vulnerability becomes publicly visible in the database and the CVE is published). Patchstack is a CVE Numbering Authority (CNA), assigning unique CVE IDs to each valid submission, with assignment potentially delayed to prevent conflicts between multiple CNAs in the WordPress ecosystem.
docs/wp-context.md:605:Monthly competitions run from the first to the last day of each month (UTC). Patchstack guarantees a minimum monthly pool of **$8,800** distributed across the top 20 researchers: 1st place earns **$2,000**, 2nd gets $1,400, 3rd gets $800, scaling down to $50 for 20th place plus one randomly selected researcher outside the top 20 who submitted at least one valid report with XP greater than zero. XP is calculated as: **CVSS base score × Active install multiplier × Privilege requirement multiplier × Vulnerability type multiplier**. No XP is awarded if the component has fewer than 500 active installs or requires admin/super-admin role, though CVE IDs are still issued.
docs/wp-context.md:619:Patchstack's bug bounty program is unusually prescriptive compared to generic programs, reflecting the specific threat landscape of WordPress's plugin ecosystem. The most consequential rules for researchers are the **blanket exclusion of admin-level vulnerabilities**, the **CVSS 5.3 floor** (reduced to 4.3 only for very popular components), and the **100-install minimum** — together, these three gates filter out the majority of potential submissions before any technical evaluation begins. The Patchstack Priority system's finding that nearly 70% of reported vulnerabilities are "theoretical" and unlikely to be exploited underscores the program's emphasis on **meaningful, real-world impact** over vulnerability volume. Recent 2024–2026 updates show a clear trajectory: higher bounties (doubled pool, Zeroday payouts up to $33,000), stricter quality controls (AI false-positive penalties, dedicated security-only patch requirements), and EU regulatory alignment through instant post-patch disclosure. Researchers maximizing their returns should focus on unauthenticated or subscriber-level vulnerabilities in plugins with 100K+ installs, prioritizing RCE, arbitrary file upload/deletion, and privilege escalation to admin — the vulnerability types that earn both the highest XP multipliers and Zeroday bounty eligibility.

